Introduction to Environmental Studies: Explains how humans, plants, animals, and natural resources are connected. Teaches nature, health, society, and responsible living.
Living and Non-Living Things: Living things (humans, animals, plants) grow, breathe, move, need food, and reproduce. Non-living things (stones, tables) do not.
Plants Around Us: Provide food, oxygen, medicine, wood, and shelter. Parts: roots, stems, leaves, flowers.
Animals and Their Homes: Lions in dens, birds in nests, cows in sheds, dogs in kennels.
Food We Eat: Energy to grow and stay healthy. Sources: plants (rice, fruits) and animals (milk, meat). Balanced diet.
Our Body and Health: Hygiene (washing hands, bathing), exercise, and sleep.
Air Around Us: Needed for breathing and burning. Pollution from vehicles and factories.
Water – Our Lifeline: Used for drinking, cooking, washing, and farming. Sources: rivers, lakes, wells, rain.
Cleanliness and Waste Management: Recycling, separating wet and dry waste.
Family, Community, and Care for Earth: Cooperation, sharing, and respect.
